Training spiking neural networks on Riemannian manifolds requires backpropagation through time (BPTT), whose memory grows linearly with the sequence length. We treat this as a three-way memory--accuracy--energy trade-off and report all three sides. Analytic Metric Correction (AMC) replaces full BPTT with truncated backpropagation plus an analytic correction of the Riemannian metric factor, giving $O(M)$ stored states: fourteen times fewer states at $T{=}128$. We prove the gradient error is bounded by $C(1-\rho^{T-M})$ with $C$ uniformly bounded in $T$, and confirm the bound's shape on the real model (maximum relative error $6.3\times10^{-4}$ over 56 units; exact agreement with full BPTT at $M{=}T$) without claiming tightness. The accuracy side is measured rather than assumed: AMC is 5.5 points below full BPTT on MUTAG ($p{=}0.048$) and 6.6 points below the no-correction control, reaching $74.0\%$ against $53.8\%$ for the MSG baseline and $85.6\%$ for a dense ANN encoder. Under an analytical energy model the spiking model uses $89$--$99.2\%$ less \emph{estimated} energy, yet the STMP stage is silent on three of four datasets, where accuracy collapses to chance. A mechanism result bounds what the correction can deliver: it telescopes exactly to the endpoint ratio $\lambda_0^2/\lambda_T^2$ and is near-identity once trajectories reach a stationary norm (median $|\Delta\cos|=3\times10^{-6}$ over 260 measurements). We withdraw an earlier claim of 46-percentage-point degradation from removing the correction.
